Condition : 4 - No development shall commence until a Construction and Environmental Management Plan (CEMP) has been submitted to and been approved in writing by the local planning authority. The CEMP shall include measures to mitigate the construction effects as part of a coordinated and collaborative approach with surrounding developments, and shall include the following details (although not limited to):
- whilst works should take place outside of the nesting season (March September inclusive), if works to the building and/or onsite vegetation are unavoidable during this period, then a site walk over within 48 hours prior to site clearance is required by a named CIEEM accredited ecologist with experience of working in London to check for any nesting birds in the vegetation and buildings. In the event that a nest is found, an appropriate exclusion zone should be implemented around it until the young have fully fledged (Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 (as amended)). The law protects all wild bird species, their eggs and nests;
- details of a precautionary bat mitigation plan, with details of sensitive working methods including the soft stripping of potential roost features and a bat tool box talk;
- whilst lighting should be avoided, if lighting is necessary, details of a construction lighting plan outlining how lighting will be prevented from negatively impacting any protected and priority species during construction, in particular commuting bats. This construction lighting plan should follow the best practice industry guidance outlined in the Bat Conservation Trust (BCT) and Institute of Lighting Professionals (ILP) Guidance Note (2023);
- details of measures to prevent entrapment of or harm to wildlife on site, including in excavations and construction and waste materials left on site;
- details of an Invasive Non-Native Species plan detailing the measures to be taken to remove and eradicate any INNS (including species on the London Invasive Species Initiative (LISI) lis

Condition : 6 - Notwithstanding the details shown on the approved plans and set out in the supporting information, Landscape and Ecological Enhancement and Management Plan shall be submitted to and approved in writing by the local planning authority prior to the commencement of any above ground works. The Plan shall include details of all measures to provide biodiversity gain, including but not limited to:
- details of all proposed planting including species and plans, with priority given to native and wildlife friendly planting;
- details of biodiverse roofs (including Biosolar roofs), including details of extensive substrate base, including where the substrate is from, planting/seeding, with priority given to wildlife friendly species and features to be included within the substrate e.g. rope coils, sand, gravel, etc.(in accordance with The Gro Green Roof Code 2021);
- orientation, target species and location for at least 2x swift boxes/bricks and 2x bat box/ brick to be integrated with the buildings;
- details of hedgehog holes in fences to allow connectivity between neighbouring gardens including appropriate signage;
- details of log piles and deadwood features;
- if lighting is required, a bat sensitive post-construction lighting plan to ensure that any lighting will be of a specification that minimises its impacts on bats and any onsite habitats as well as adjacent habitats in accordance with the BCT and ILP 2023 Guidance Note (including having a colour temperature of <2700k, no uplighters and no light spill onto ecological enhancements or habitats).
- details on the management to be implemented post-construction shall also be submitted, which shall include management responsibilities and maintenance schedules for all hard and soft landscaped areas, any BNG related habitat, as well as the above-mentioned measures to provide biodiversity gain.

The approved details shall be implemented prior to first occupation of the development and maintained thereafter.
